{
  "id": "factorial-trailing-zeroes",
  "questionId": "factorial-trailing-zeroes",
  "slug": "factorial-trailing-zeroes",
  "title": "Factorial Trailing Zeroes",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Greedy",
  "patterns": [
    "math",
    "greedy"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "a trailing zero comes from a factor of 10, and 10 = 2 x 5 - so the number of trailing zeroes equals the number of times 10 divides n! evenly",
    "in the product 1 x 2 x ... x n, factors of 2 are far more plentiful than factors of 5 (every other number contributes a 2, only every fifth contributes a 5), so the count of trailing zeroes is limited entirely by how many factors of 5 appear",
    "count how many multiples of 5 are <= n, that's n / 5 (integer division) - but numbers like 25 contribute two factors of 5, not just one",
    "sum n/5 + n/25 + n/125 + ... (integer division, stopping once the divisor exceeds n) to account for numbers that contribute multiple factors of 5"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "computing n! directly with big integers, which is correct but far too slow and memory-heavy for large n",
    "counting only n / 5 and forgetting that multiples of 25, 125, etc. contribute additional factors of 5",
    "counting factors of 2 instead of 5 (factors of 2 are never the bottleneck)",
    "using a division that overflows or loops incorrectly when the power of 5 exceeds n"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an integer `n`. `n!` (n factorial) is the product of every\ninteger from 1 to `n`.\n\n**Return the number of trailing zeroes in `n!`.**\n\nYour solution should not actually compute `n!` itself, since it grows far too large to hold in memory for even moderately sized `n`.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= n <= 10^4",
      "the answer fits comfortably in a 32-bit integer",
      "do not literally compute n! and count zeroes on it"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 or n = 1 (0! = 1! = 1, zero trailing zeroes)",
      "n is itself a large power of 5, contributing more than one factor of 5",
      "n just below a multiple of 5 (e.g. n = 24), where the count doesn't increase from the previous value"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
